    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where Wix is genuinely fine</h2>
    <p>I am not going to pretend Wix is a bad product. It is a polished, well-marketed website builder. Specifically:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You want to launch this weekend.</strong> Wix lets a non-technical owner stand up a working site in a day. A custom build takes 2&ndash;3 weeks.</li>
      <li><strong>You want to edit the site yourself.</strong> Wix's drag-and-drop editor is genuinely easy. If you plan to make weekly changes by hand, Wix is built for that workflow. With me, you email me changes and I do them; if you prefer the drag-and-drop loop, Wix wins.</li>
      <li><strong>You are still figuring out your business.</strong> Spending $4,000 on a website for a business model you might pivot in three months is poor capital allocation. Wix at $30/month buys you time.</li>
      <li><strong>You have a strong brand identity already and just need a placeholder.</strong> Wix has decent templates if your visual standards are forgiving and you do not need to look unique.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where Wix hurts you</h2>
    <p>The cost is not the monthly subscription. The cost is what Wix costs you in things you cannot easily measure: speed, search ranking, ownership, and brand differentiation. Specifically:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Page speed is structurally worse.</strong> Wix sites typically score 50&ndash;70 on Google PageSpeed mobile. A custom-coded site scores 95&ndash;100. The difference is not a config setting; it is the architecture (Wix bundles every visitor's page through their layout engine, every visit). Page speed is a Google ranking factor and a conversion factor &mdash; faster sites rank higher and convert more visitors into calls.</li>
      <li><strong>You do not own the site.</strong> Stop paying Wix and the site is gone. There is no "export to a real host" path. The HTML, CSS, and content are entangled with the Wix runtime. With a custom-coded site, you own the code (lump-sum) or own the static HTML/CSS/JS export (monthly) &mdash; either way it runs on any host.</li>
      <li><strong>Local SEO is shallower.</strong> Wix's structured data is generic. Service-area pages built in Wix often look like every other Wix service-area page, which Google's local algorithm increasingly penalizes as low-effort doorways. Custom-coded sites get hand-written schema that matches your actual Google Business Profile.</li>
      <li><strong>Your site looks like the template.</strong> Wix's "starter templates" power tens of thousands of sites. Your competitors are using the same ones. Brand differentiation is hard to achieve when the editor's defaults shape every page.</li>
      <li><strong>The forever-subscription trap.</strong> $30&ndash;$50 a month seems cheap. Over five years that is $1,800&ndash;$3,000 paid for the same website that is structurally limited. With a custom build at $4,000 lump-sum, you spend that once and own the result.</li>
    </ul>

